2. Global background system
The background is one of the most important parts of the entire design.

It is not a flat black background. It uses a layered photographic and gradient-based composition.

The background creates the impression of standing inside a dark, sophisticated agricultural environment.
2.1 Base background
The foundation is an extremely dark green-black surface.

Suggested CSS:
body {
    margin: 0;
    min-height: 100vh;

    background-color: #03100c;

    color: #f1f7f3;

    font-family: "Inter", sans-serif;

    overflow-x: hidden;
}


The dominant background colors include:
Color	Purpose
#020907	Deepest black-green
#03130E	Main background
#061C13	Dark green atmospheric areas
#092719	Subtle green illumination
#102D1B	Background foliage
#183B22	Brighter foliage highlights

2.2 Background photography
Large, darkened agricultural photographs are positioned behind the dashboard.

The photographs include:
- Oversized green leaves.
- Cropped tropical foliage.
- Agricultural landscapes.
- Subtle green vegetation.
- Dark natural textures.
The most prominent foliage is visible across the upper section of the screenshot.

There is a large green leaf extending diagonally from approximately the upper-middle region toward the upper-right region.

Another broad leaf appears around the upper-right corner.

These leaves are deliberately oversized and cropped by the viewport boundaries.

The photographic imagery must not interfere with the legibility of the dashboard.
Background implementation
Use several independent layers:
.dashboard {
    position: relative;
    isolation: isolate;
    min-height: 100vh;

    background:
        radial-gradient(
            ellipse at 52% 15%,
            rgba(12, 90, 45, 0.16),
            transparent 40%
        ),
        radial-gradient(
            ellipse at 0% 70%,
            rgba(31, 112, 49, 0.17),
            transparent 35%
        ),
        linear-gradient(
            120deg,
            #06180f,
            #020b08 55%,
            #03140d
        );
}


The photographic foliage should be placed in absolutely positioned background layers, using object-fit: cover.

A final dark overlay should be placed above the photographs.

Important: The photographs must remain visible as subtle environmental details. They should not become large, distracting hero images.

